3}+0{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nUsing the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{2}-6x+10 \\\\  & 2x+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,6{x}^{3}+\\phantom{1}0{x}^{2}-\\phantom{1}4x+\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,12{x}^{2}-4x+5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,12{x}^{2}-24x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,20x+5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{20x+40}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are\n\n$\\,$ $3{x}^{2}-6x+10\\,$ and $\\,20x+5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77cb842f-af82-5e3c-b273-ad897c801074"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77cb842f-af82-5e3c-b273-ad897c801074" :answer-field/id #uuid "77cb842f-af82-5e3c-b273-ad897c801074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ab9d4c04-1593-54ac-a096-03d06183bf9f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1bd98812-61b5-5ff4-9406-13a68de9fc9c" "answer-b10db460-724b-5352-814a-3b123df62556" "answer-e6788818-475c-590e-852b-e90d260074b7" "answer-2e23c518-4127-5d89-ad22-11745eab87b4" "answer-ab9d4c04-1593-54ac-a096-03d06183bf9f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bd98812-61b5-5ff4-9406-13a68de9fc9c" :answer/id #uuid "1bd98812-61b5-5ff4-9406-13a68de9fc9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3{x}^{2}+3x-5\\,$ and $\\,-5x+20$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b10db460-724b-5352-814a-3b123df62556" :answer/id #uuid "b10db460-724b-5352-814a-3b123df62556" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3{x}^{2}+6x-10\\,$ and $\\,-20x-5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6788818-475c-590e-852b-e90d260074b7" :answer/id #uuid "e6788818-475c-590e-852b-e90d260074b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3{x}^{2}-3x+5\\,$ and $\\,20x-5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e23c518-4127-5d89-ad22-11745eab87b4" :answer/id #uuid "2e23c518-4127-5d89-ad22-11745eab87b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3{x}^{2}-6x+10\\,$ and $\\,10x+5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab9d4c04-1593-54ac-a096-03d06183bf9f" :answer/id #uuid "ab9d4c04-1593-54ac-a096-03d06183bf9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3{x}^{2}-6x+10\\,$ and $\\,20x+5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186399823 :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & x-6\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,6{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}0x-\\phantom{00}4} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{2}-36x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{36x-216}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,212\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$-term. So, we write it using a zero coefficient, as follows: \n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+0x-4\n$$\n\nUsing the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x+36 \\\\  & x-6\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,6{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}0x-\\phantom{00}4} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{2}-36x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,36x-4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{36x-216}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,212\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are\n\n$\\,$ $6x+36\\,$ and $\\,36x-4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e5d7e360-3d1a-54c3-a890-be5694ec9bb1" :question/id #uuid "e5d7e360-3d1a-54c3-a890-be5694ec9bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37965" :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & 2x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+7x+0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x+0 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\boxed{}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the dividend is missing the constant term. So, we write it using a zero coefficient, as follows:\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+7x+0\n$$\n\nUsing the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+3\\, \\\\  & 2x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+7x+0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x+0 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6x+3}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are\n\n$\\,$ $x+3\\,$ and $\\,6x+3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-182693e0-0012-5b4d-9614-71a4b11bfc11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-182693e0-0012-5b4d-9614-71a4b11bfc11" :answer-field/id #uuid "182693e0-0012-5b4d-9614-71a4b11bfc11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-37a69f9e-088b-562d-91eb-cd8f8d33d181" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1fe4db4b-48ee-5078-99d2-c89a9a1e786a" "answer-00690689-3ae4-5a92-bc2e-e97364d44b87" "answer-37a69f9e-088b-562d-91eb-cd8f8d33d181" "answer-54550716-4af7-538c-8f3d-c31c24df9c53" "answer-d507c352-679c-50e6-8ae7-473b8d757a25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fe4db4b-48ee-5078-99d2-c89a9a1e786a" :answer/id #uuid "1fe4db4b-48ee-5078-99d2-c89a9a1e786a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x+1\\,$ and $\\,3x+2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00690689-3ae4-5a92-bc2e-e97364d44b87" :answer/id #uuid "00690689-3ae4-5a92-bc2e-e97364d44b87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x+3\\,$ and $\\,3x+6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37a69f9e-088b-562d-91eb-cd8f8d33d181" :answer/id #uuid "37a69f9e-088b-562d-91eb-cd8f8d33d181" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x+3\\,$ and $\\,6x+3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54550716-4af7-538c-8f3d-c31c24df9c53" :answer/id #uuid "54550716-4af7-538c-8f3d-c31c24df9c53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x-1\\,$ and $\\,2x+1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d507c352-679c-50e6-8ae7-473b8d757a25" :answer/id #uuid "d507c352-679c-50e6-8ae7-473b8d757a25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x-3\\,$ and $\\,6x+3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ad64733e-6511-5f51-b164-9b0ad359ab1e" :question/id #uuid "ad64733e-6511-5f51-b164-9b0ad359ab1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-38112" :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & x-7\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,7{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}0x-\\phantom{00}6} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{7{x}^{2}-49x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{49x-343}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,337\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$-term. So, we write it using a zero coefficient, as follows:\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}+0x-6\n$$\n\nUsing the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,7x+49 \\\\  & x-7\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,7{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}0x-\\phantom{00}6} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{7{x}^{2}-49x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,49x-6 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{49x-343}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,337\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are\n\n$\\,$ $7x+49\\,$ and $\\,49x-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc476439-d624-5e0c-b41e-7cea26dc47b1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc476439-d624-5e0c-b41e-7cea26dc47b1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc476439-d624-5e0c-b41e-7cea26dc47b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05fb0fd1-4f91-57c9-9d97-59b378fa032e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8714f9d8-0aeb-569c-bc14-f4d10d14a5e5" "answer-05fb0fd1-4f91-57c9-9d97-59b378fa032e" "answer-218d462b-d6f8-5a2c-8cce-b48fe895db83" "answer-566bc607-57aa-57b9-a3c9-0ee36ce6ebc8" "answer-ee24db36-8bfa-5be2-9784-f61aed7f16ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8714f9d8-0aeb-569c-bc14-f4d10d14a5e5" :answer/id #uuid "8714f9d8-0aeb-569c-bc14-f4d10d14a5e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7x+49\\,$ and $\\,49x-343$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05fb0fd1-4f91-57c9-9d97-59b378fa032e" :answer/id #uuid "05fb0fd1-4f91-57c9-9d97-59b378fa032e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7x+49\\,$ and $\\,49x-6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-218d462b-d6f8-5a2c-8cce-b48fe895db83" :answer/id #uuid "218d462b-d6f8-5a2c-8cce-b48fe895db83" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7x+7\\,$ and $\\,7x-42$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-566bc607-57aa-57b9-a3c9-0ee36ce6ebc8" :answer/id #uuid "566bc607-57aa-57b9-a3c9-0ee36ce6ebc8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7x-49\\,$ and $\\,-49x+6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee24db36-8bfa-5be2-9784-f61aed7f16ce" :answer/id #uuid "ee24db36-8bfa-5be2-9784-f61aed7f16ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7x-49\\,$ and $\\,-49x-6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130415 :tutorial/content "Our task is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In this case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to begin.\n\nTo carry out the division, we follow the following step-by-step process.\n\n **Step 1** \n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n **Step 2** \n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}2x^2})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}2x^2}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}2x}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}{({\\color{blue}{x}}+5)}={2x^2+10x}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract ${2x^2+10x}$ from ${2x^2+8x - 5}$ and get ${{-2x-5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3** \n\nWe now repeat the process. The dividend is now ${{\\color{red}{-2x}}-5}.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}-2x})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}-2x}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}$ term we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}({{\\color{blue}{x}}+5})={-2x-10}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract ${{-2x-10}}$ from ${{-2x-5}}$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're left with the constant ${\\color{red}{5}}.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is $\\color{blue}{2x-2},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129356 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119841 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bf386a8a-8501-5e3e-a330-283d65ae0e3a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bf386a8a-8501-5e3e-a330-283d65ae0e3a" :question/id #uuid "bf386a8a-8501-5e3e-a330-283d65ae0e3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129154" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,3,7)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-2x-3y+6z=16.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-2$ and $y=0$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-3y+6z & =16 \\\\ -2(-2)-3(0)+6z & =16 \\\\ 6z & =12 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-2,0,2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-2,0,2⟩-⟨5,3,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,-3,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-2,-3,6⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-7)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+(-5)\\cdot 6|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(6)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|14+9-30|}{\\sqrt{4+9+36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-7|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-adece7a5-0744-52d2-a422-6e0bf364c79d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-adece7a5-0744-52d2-a422-6e0bf364c79d" :answer-field/id #uuid "adece7a5-0744-52d2-a422-6e0bf364c79d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc5a4b5d-4377-54c9-bfe2-c832ba673c98" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dc5a4b5d-4377-54c9-bfe2-c832ba673c98" "answer-3016c831-e8f4-5651-b74c-6377c71dd95f" "answer-1e1b5af0-a05c-505e-b5bf-10afc44fa4fd" "answer-4275d3b1-6448-558a-b4a5-a4d62b2ac4d8" "answer-cebc6772-d1ae-5b50-bd1a-7a34f67a24f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc5a4b5d-4377-54c9-bfe2-c832ba673c98" :answer/id #uuid "dc5a4b5d-4377-54c9-bfe2-c832ba673c98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3016c831-e8f4-5651-b74c-6377c71dd95f" :answer/id #uuid "3016c831-e8f4-5651-b74c-6377c71dd95f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e1b5af0-a05c-505e-b5bf-10afc44fa4fd" :answer/id #uuid "1e1b5af0-a05c-505e-b5bf-10afc44fa4fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4275d3b1-6448-558a-b4a5-a4d62b2ac4d8" :answer/id #uuid "4275d3b1-6448-558a-b4a5-a4d62b2ac4d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cebc6772-d1ae-5b50-bd1a-7a34f67a24f9" :answer/id #uuid "cebc6772-d1ae-5b50-bd1a-7a34f67a24f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-607e9414-4918-5745-b10c-99f76f09cb79" :activity/id #uuid "607e9414-4918-5745-b10c-99f76f09cb79" :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049322 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-fcc02eae-d041-5167-9e1c-3e49457a629e" "step-40058c5d-013f-5ce7-9c88-237da66a5575" "step-5bfda559-d502-5665-9d67-d803ab540b42" "step-c4143c0c-c3e3-5771-b847-04afefbdec44" "step-14537fa3-6b22-5ad7-8a11-c7c69ec06d24"] :activity/first-step "step-fcc02eae-d041-5167-9e1c-3e49457a629e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-fcc02eae-d041-5167-9e1c-3e49457a629e" :step/id #uuid "fcc02eae-d041-5167-9e1c-3e49457a629e" :step/content 17592186315010 :step/next "step-40058c5d-013f-5ce7-9c88-237da66a5575" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-40058c5d-013f-5ce7-9c88-237da66a5575" :step/id #uuid "40058c5d-013f-5ce7-9c88-237da66a5575" :step/content 17592186262941 :step/next "step-5bfda559-d502-5665-9d67-d803ab540b42" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5bfda559-d502-5665-9d67-d803ab540b42" :step/id #uuid "5bfda559-d502-5665-9d67-d803ab540b42" :step/content 17592186262942 :step/next "step-c4143c0c-c3e3-5771-b847-04afefbdec44" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c4143c0c-c3e3-5771-b847-04afefbdec44" :step/id #uuid "c4143c0c-c3e3-5771-b847-04afefbdec44" :step/content "question-bf386a8a-8501-5e3e-a330-283d65ae0e3a" :step/next "step-14537fa3-6b22-5ad7-8a11-c7c69ec06d24" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-14537fa3-6b22-5ad7-8a11-c7c69ec06d24" :step/id #uuid "14537fa3-6b22-5ad7-8a11-c7c69ec06d24" :step/content 17592186262962 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138387 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b85f6c30-099c-5add-8b59-401be051e650" "question-697f5959-f087-5527-b15d-214bd9021ebd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138164 :question/problem "The height $h$ of a lamppost is $30\\,\\textrm{ft}$ to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{ft}.$ Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{ft}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,\\textrm{ft}}{2} = 0.5\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =30\\,\\text{ft}-0.5\\,\\text{ft} \\\\  & =29.5\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =30\\,\\text{ft}+0.5\\,\\text{ft} \\\\  & =30.5\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $h$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n29.5\\,\\textrm{ft} \\leq h <    30.5\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138388 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-813619c9-d532-5fa8-aa46-e2d238a68f09" "question-9595515f-fef9-57d0-bdc3-6db4a7b77611"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138165 :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\textrm{km}^2.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.1\\,\\textrm{km}^2}{2} = 0.05\\,\\textrm{km}^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =0.6\\,{\\text{km}}^{2}-0.05\\,{\\text{km}}^{2} \\\\  & =0.55\\,{\\text{km}}^{2} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =0.6\\,{\\text{km}}^{2}+0.05\\,{\\text{km}}^{2} \\\\  & =0.65\\,{\\text{km}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $a$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.55\\,\\textrm{km}^2 \\leq a <            0.65\\,\\textrm{km}^2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138389 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f2d8e097-556a-5209-9bb4-f4ab8f864a87" "question-f23b7a04-be79-58bf-9d3e-f31e5bea28da"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138167 :question/problem "The dimensions of a parking lot are $90\\, \\textrm{m} \\times 50\\, \\textrm{m}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $10\\, \\textrm{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest ten square meters, what is the upper bound for the area of the parking lot?" :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{m}}{2} = 5\\,\\textrm{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *upper bound* for the area of the parking lot. Therefore, we require the *upper bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe upper bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 90\\,\\text{m}+5\\,\\text{m}=95\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 50\\,\\text{m}+5\\,\\text{m}=55\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the upper bound for the area of the parking lot is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =95\\,\\text{m}\\times 55\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =5\\,225 \\\\  & ≈5\\,230\\,{\\text{m}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten square meters." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b85f6c30-099c-5add-8b59-401be051e650" :question/id #uuid "b85f6c30-099c-5add-8b59-401be051e650" :question/math-academy-id "q-154066" :question/problem "The length $l$ of a knife blade is $14\\,\\text{cm}$ to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{cm}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =14\\,\\text{cm}-0.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =13.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =14\\,\\text{cm}+0.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =14.5\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $l$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n13.5\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<14.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f878e921-2acc-50fd-8e43-f4ed513a9b8e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f878e921-2acc-50fd-8e43-f4ed513a9b8e" :answer-field/id #uuid "f878e921-2acc-50fd-8e43-f4ed513a9b8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-71967f4e-bf57-5679-af0f-b50e405893c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-71967f4e-bf57-5679-af0f-b50e405893c4" "answer-7370fc6e-00e4-5423-8124-b0631c2aabd5" "answer-74bf6f74-26df-55de-8361-fc78879d0972" "answer-50ae9cc8-39d1-55e5-be52-6173a02aa0cb" "answer-b26726e6-ef31-50f9-aa7f-af28f0df4788"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71967f4e-bf57-5679-af0f-b50e405893c4" :answer/id #uuid "71967f4e-bf57-5679-af0f-b50e405893c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13.5\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<14.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7370fc6e-00e4-5423-8124-b0631c2aabd5" :answer/id #uuid "7370fc6e-00e4-5423-8124-b0631c2aabd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13.95\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<14.05\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74bf6f74-26df-55de-8361-fc78879d0972" :answer/id #uuid "74bf6f74-26df-55de-8361-fc78879d0972" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13.9\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<14.1\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50ae9cc8-39d1-55e5-be52-6173a02aa0cb" :answer/id #uuid "50ae9cc8-39d1-55e5-be52-6173a02aa0cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b26726e6-ef31-50f9-aa7f-af28f0df4788" :answer/id #uuid "b26726e6-ef31-50f9-aa7f-af28f0df4788" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<24\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-697f5959-f087-5527-b15d-214bd9021ebd" :question/id #uuid "697f5959-f087-5527-b15d-214bd9021ebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-154064" :question/problem "The distance $d$ between two lampposts is $40\\,\\text{m}$ to the nearest $10\\,\\text{m}.$ Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $10\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{10\\,\\text{m}}{2}=5\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =40\\,\\text{m}-5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =35\\,\\text{m} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =40\\,\\text{m}+5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =45\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $d$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n35\\,\\text{m}\\le d<45\\,\\text{m}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f5a10c85-4b92-5a7f-9aa4-97e39df01cc3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f5a10c85-4b92-5a7f-9aa4-97e39df01cc3" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a10c85-4b92-5a7f-9aa4-97e39df01cc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-512e9253-a4a7-5c0b-97ea-83527ff1f3fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-56138ea0-d350-5323-832f-ed4b6da5287d" "answer-90513a05-a76e-52b5-9628-feaba6b47d86" "answer-512e9253-a4a7-5c0b-97ea-83527ff1f3fb" "answer-f038b6fc-e31f-5379-89d5-d85f9d5e491a" "answer-d5e4f648-784b-5a43-87ac-77da3ef5f518"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56138ea0-d350-5323-832f-ed4b6da5287d" :answer/id #uuid "56138ea0-d350-5323-832f-ed4b6da5287d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30\\,\\text{m}\\le d<50\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90513a05-a76e-52b5-9628-feaba6b47d86" :answer/id #uuid "90513a05-a76e-52b5-9628-feaba6b47d86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35.5\\,\\text{m}\\le d<44.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-512e9253-a4a7-5c0b-97ea-83527ff1f3fb" :answer/id #uuid "512e9253-a4a7-5c0b-97ea-83527ff1f3fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35\\,\\text{m}\\le d<45\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f038b6fc-e31f-5379-89d5-d85f9d5e491a" :answer/id #uuid "f038b6fc-e31f-5379-89d5-d85f9d5e491a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "39.5\\,\\text{m}\\le d<40.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5e4f648-784b-5a43-87ac-77da3ef5f518" :answer/id #uuid "d5e4f648-784b-5a43-87ac-77da3ef5f518" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "39\\,\\text{m}\\le d<41\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-813619c9-d532-5fa8-aa46-e2d238a68f09" :question/id #uuid "813619c9-d532-5fa8-aa46-e2d238a68f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-218706" :question/problem "The time $t$ it took Noah to run $200\\,\\text{m}$ was $31.2\\,\\text{s}$ to the nearest tenth of a second. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement.\n\n{{field-1}}$\\text{s}$\n$\\,\\le t<$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{s}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{s}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =31.2\\,\\text{s}-0.05\\,\\text{s} \\\\  & =31.15\\,\\text{s} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =31.2\\,\\text{s}+0.05\\,\\text{s} \\\\  & =31.25\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $t$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n31.15\\,\\text{s}\\le t<31.25\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1a84bda0-ecde-520d-a425-82bf05a6c34f" "answer-field-e734ae0c-9ed5-5bce-b1fc-753baadcafc6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a84bda0-ecde-520d-a425-82bf05a6c34f" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a84bda0-ecde-520d-a425-82bf05a6c34f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-197aacc1-a89c-56b5-bbfc-363af67376d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-197aacc1-a89c-56b5-bbfc-363af67376d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-197aacc1-a89c-56b5-bbfc-363af67376d6" :answer/id #uuid "197aacc1-a89c-56b5-bbfc-363af67376d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "31.15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e734ae0c-9ed5-5bce-b1fc-753baadcafc6" :answer-field/id #uuid "e734ae0c-9ed5-5bce-b1fc-753baadcafc6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-52390098-dc96-539b-af82-ac72adda1cf9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52390098-dc96-539b-af82-ac72adda1cf9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52390098-dc96-539b-af82-ac72adda1cf9" :answer/id #uuid "52390098-dc96-539b-af82-ac72adda1cf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "31.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9595515f-fef9-57d0-bdc3-6db4a7b77611" :question/id #uuid "9595515f-fef9-57d0-bdc3-6db4a7b77611" :question/math-academy-id "q-218713" :question/problem "The distance $d$ between the easternmost and westernmost points of the contiguous United States measures $2,693.1\\,\\text{mi}$ to the nearest tenth of a mile. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement.\n\n{{field-1}}$\\text{mi}$\n$\\,\\le d<$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{mi}$" :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{mi}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{mi}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{mi}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =2,693.1\\,\\text{mi}-0.05\\,\\text{mi} \\\\  & =2,693.05\\,\\text{mi} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =2,693.1\\,\\text{mi}+0.05\\,\\text{mi} \\\\  & =2,693.15\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $d$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2,693.05\\,\\text{mi}\\le d<2,693.15\\,\\text{mi}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72266152-ce5d-5351-b6cb-62c0f80e6040" "answer-field-a37eb16d-22ea-52e4-acfe-081207b7a264"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72266152-ce5d-5351-b6cb-62c0f80e6040" :answer-field/id #uuid "72266152-ce5d-5351-b6cb-62c0f80e6040" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4d28e809-0be7-5de5-b7b6-efed5550afd2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d28e809-0be7-5de5-b7b6-efed5550afd2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d28e809-0be7-5de5-b7b6-efed5550afd2" :answer/id #uuid "4d28e809-0be7-5de5-b7b6-efed5550afd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2693.05" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a37eb16d-22ea-52e4-acfe-081207b7a264" :answer-field/id #uuid "a37eb16d-22ea-52e4-acfe-081207b7a264" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6c54b4dd-d42b-596e-9a9b-87b3dcaa4810" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6c54b4dd-d42b-596e-9a9b-87b3dcaa4810"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c54b4dd-d42b-596e-9a9b-87b3dcaa4810" :answer/id #uuid "6c54b4dd-d42b-596e-9a9b-87b3dcaa4810" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2693.15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2d8e097-556a-5209-9bb4-f4ab8f864a87" :question/id #uuid "f2d8e097-556a-5209-9bb4-f4ab8f864a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-46382" :question/problem "The dimensions of the cover of a chemistry book are $28\\,\\text{cm}\\times 14\\,\\text{cm}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ Rounded to the nearest square centimeter, what is the smallest possible area of the book's cover?" :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{cm}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible area of the book's cover. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 28\\,\\text{cm}-0.5\\,\\text{cm}=27.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & 14\\,\\text{cm}-0.5\\,\\text{cm}=13.5\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  the smallest possible area of the book's cover is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =27.5\\,\\text{cm}\\times 13.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =371.25\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈371\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest square centimeter." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d98b6691-4a9c-574e-8a86-2da84cb14cd2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d98b6691-4a9c-574e-8a86-2da84cb14cd2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d98b6691-4a9c-574e-8a86-2da84cb14cd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-18868649-9176-55f0-bcee-dc4acd091801" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d77b9e5-d60d-55dd-ba48-ad825b552253" "answer-1acae981-193e-5375-b7d3-e5c7dc725228" "answer-18868649-9176-55f0-bcee-dc4acd091801" "answer-ea4ebd3d-80cd-55b5-bd9b-475e7e2224d9" "answer-b96546a2-8483-5d95-a192-078c54ead42b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d77b9e5-d60d-55dd-ba48-ad825b552253" :answer/id #uuid "4d77b9e5-d60d-55dd-ba48-ad825b552253" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "364\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1acae981-193e-5375-b7d3-e5c7dc725228" :answer/id #uuid "1acae981-193e-5375-b7d3-e5c7dc725228" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "368\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18868649-9176-55f0-bcee-dc4acd091801" :answer/id #uuid "18868649-9176-55f0-bcee-dc4acd091801" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "371\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea4ebd3d-80cd-55b5-bd9b-475e7e2224d9" :answer/id #uuid "ea4ebd3d-80cd-55b5-bd9b-475e7e2224d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "375\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b96546a2-8483-5d95-a192-078c54ead42b" :answer/id #uuid "b96546a2-8483-5d95-a192-078c54ead42b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "383\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f23b7a04-be79-58bf-9d3e-f31e5bea28da" :question/id #uuid "f23b7a04-be79-58bf-9d3e-f31e5bea28da" :question/math-academy-id "q-218721" :question/problem "The dimensions of a stamp are $2.1\\,\\text{cm}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{cm}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{cm}.$ Rounded to the nearest tenth of a square centimeter, what is the upper bound for the area of the stamp?\n\nTo the nearest tenth of a square centimeter, the upper bound is\n{{field-1}}${\\text{cm}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{cm}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *upper bound* for the area of the stamp. Therefore, we require the *upper bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe upper bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2.1\\,\\text{cm}+0.05\\,\\text{cm}=2.15\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{cm}+0.05\\,\\text{cm}=2.45\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the upper bound for the area of the stamp is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2.15\\,\\text{cm}\\times 2.45\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =5.267\\,5\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈5.3\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a square centimeter." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c5f8348-62fb-52f4-8bc5-1b6a23724307"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c5f8348-62fb-52f4-8bc5-1b6a23724307" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c5f8348-62fb-52f4-8bc5-1b6a23724307" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-450ed54a-6b67-51e3-97f3-9938e38a763d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-450ed54a-6b67-51e3-97f3-9938e38a763d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-450ed54a-6b67-51e3-97f3-9938e38a763d" :answer/id #uuid "450ed54a-6b67-51e3-97f3-9938e38a763d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186139715 :tutorial/content "We always introduce **errors** in our measurements whenever we round or approximate numbers. In this lesson, we will explore how to quantify and describe measurement errors.\n\nSuppose that a particular rope measures precisely $48.9\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ Now, imagine we take a meterstick and record a measurement of $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ *to the nearest $10$ centimeters*. Let's mark this approximation on our meterstick.\n\n![](images/4e/4e5d5ca05f27aa612b3fdcc9430b495870ddf540630857b6227897d6b20c9e89.png)\n\nThe error of a measurement equals the difference between the rounded estimate and the \"true\" value. So, in this example, the error of our approximation equals\n\n$$\n50\\,\\textrm{cm} - 48.9\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1.1\\textrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe often don't know the \"true\" measurement in practical problems. So, the next best thing is to determine a **lower bound** and an **upper bound** for our measurement:\n\n- The *lower* bound of a measurement is its *smallest* possible value.\n\n- The *upper* bound of a measurement is its *largest* possible value.\n\nLet's figure out the lower and upper bounds of our $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ approximation:\n\n- The *lower bound* is $45 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is the *smallest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/da/da9cd6208cef81c6fe34fbba9b4271fa3ce1f30df69eb3c631ce6fea4f12e3a3.png)\n\n- The *upper bound* is $55 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is (almost) the *largest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/23/23a195d88326b587e1864ef04e7b05f74d675f1ddc987baa2cf586af599eb4a9.png)\n\nLet's use the letter $l$ to denote the \"true\" length of the rope. Then, we can combine our lower and upper bound measurements into a single statement, as follows:\n\n$$\n45\\,\\textrm{cm} \\leq l \\lt 55\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used a *strict* \"<\" inequality for the upper bound. We cannot include $55\\textrm{cm}$ in our set of values because this number rounds to $60\\,\\textrm{cm},$ not $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nWe can visualize the lower and upper bounds on our diagram as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fdacb0787e8316a78da1ad65aa177e9a6f29215fa0d53aef88b0eb33326218d.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186139716 :tutorial/content "So, we've seen how to calculate lower and upper bounds. Before we consider another example, note the following:\n\n- The lower and upper bound depend on our approximation's **degree of accuracy**.\n\n- In the last example, the degree of accuracy was $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$ because the rope was measured to the nearest $10$ centimeters.\n\n- If we change the degree of accuracy, the lower and upper bounds will also change. So, for example, if we measured the rope to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ the lower and upper bounds would be different.\n\n- The **greatest possible error** is the largest amount the \"true\" value can be from the approximation.\n\n- This greatest possible error is always one-half of the degree of accuracy. So, in the last example, the greatest possible error in our measurement was\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{cm}}{2} = {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the greatest possible error to compute the lower and upper bounds quickly:\n\n- To compute the lower bound of an approximation, we *subtract* the greatest possible error from the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{lower bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} - {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 45\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\n- To compute the upper bound of an approximation, we *add* the greatest possible error to the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{upper bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} + {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 55\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138593 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186134302 :question/answer-fields 17592186131851] {:db/id 17592186133790 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d44c068e-9411-5bce-8330-3db6fe011439" "question-64c9e9e4-8caa-5c03-83b8-776b582c6427"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133599 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace spanned by ![](images/de/de1265737b861717c036023835039ec51f2b0e682b05f7fb4f871f90948e6c70.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix $P_S = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T$ maps any vector $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, ![](images/80/8083fd67a32cdda3cc5f409f3aa0a4748bf85f131379e408c3dca80f9584501e.svg) Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (14)^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133860 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-db652323-dfa8-59de-a50e-77465a8c2c78" "question-626db706-0c71-502f-b60c-7278dc1c0430" "question-d4d7063b-5280-591e-8d20-baad0b800149"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133676 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v} + t\\mathbf{w}$, where\n![](images/79/79765ab48a76c8f3d5ed1f4c35be519cf0f928c0622e3b7a0319b522395ccb86.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix $P_S = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T$ maps any vector $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w}$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}.$ \nHence, we have \n![](images/7d/7d7d41e02a65634517225adc2736cef6ebce4056bb6feca1ac3b9e9e865c7927.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133791 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0c114067-07c6-555d-99fb-074212f0d3a4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133600 :question/problem "Which of the following are orthogonal projection matrices?\n\n![](images/13/13213fe5fbeaaa288ba5dadeadf50e24d8b285f4641c3291c90b45352249f8ec.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133792 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4aada158-9af1-5a2f-91c3-b0f650c76290"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133601 :question/problem "![](images/66/662db584d066906126cb4e247ccd39e286a46bad59a383931dca8a35cdbcb53d.svg)\nGiven that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ above spans $S^\\perp,$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of $p_{13} + p_{32}?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d44c068e-9411-5bce-8330-3db6fe011439" :question/id #uuid "d44c068e-9411-5bce-8330-3db6fe011439" :question/math-academy-id "q-95144" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(29)^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{29}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b8cb42c8-cc80-517b-aba1-25f19a758d67"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8cb42c8-cc80-517b-aba1-25f19a758d67" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8cb42c8-cc80-517b-aba1-25f19a758d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3da1724a-5fc8-584e-ba86-e589491e4396" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c80634f-213c-5768-956c-b5aa3e43751c" "answer-3c5c2bee-dc17-546e-9bf3-1778729d605f" "answer-6aa3c620-3446-541b-a727-c6eb0e1bb98a" "answer-650f38a4-9865-554c-a8f0-56093232eb20" "answer-3da1724a-5fc8-584e-ba86-e589491e4396"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c80634f-213c-5768-956c-b5aa3e43751c" :answer/id #uuid "0c80634f-213c-5768-956c-b5aa3e43751c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c5c2bee-dc17-546e-9bf3-1778729d605f" :answer/id #uuid "3c5c2bee-dc17-546e-9bf3-1778729d605f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6aa3c620-3446-541b-a727-c6eb0e1bb98a" :answer/id #uuid "6aa3c620-3446-541b-a727-c6eb0e1bb98a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-650f38a4-9865-554c-a8f0-56093232eb20" :answer/id #uuid "650f38a4-9865-554c-a8f0-56093232eb20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{29}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3da1724a-5fc8-584e-ba86-e589491e4396" :answer/id #uuid "3da1724a-5fc8-584e-ba86-e589491e4396" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{29}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-64c9e9e4-8caa-5c03-83b8-776b582c6427" :question/id #uuid "64c9e9e4-8caa-5c03-83b8-776b582c6427" :question/math-academy-id "q-140509" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(6)^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b329a1a4-4323-5b77-9147-3852570c241b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b329a1a4-4323-5b77-9147-3852570c241b" :answer-field/id #uuid "b329a1a4-4323-5b77-9147-3852570c241b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8731c983-97f9-53c1-8f4f-50deb4aa8e18" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a3cf2d32-2b4e-55f9-9bde-734436c49d05" "answer-e4d68383-0e6b-5caf-9485-668de5347ff7" "answer-af3cc332-417c-564a-9706-84c0a8c01993" "answer-8731c983-97f9-53c1-8f4f-50deb4aa8e18" "answer-7172c2f1-854e-5f7a-bb0a-b29292218c1d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3cf2d32-2b4e-55f9-9bde-734436c49d05" :answer/id #uuid "a3cf2d32-2b4e-55f9-9bde-734436c49d05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4d68383-0e6b-5caf-9485-668de5347ff7" :answer/id #uuid "e4d68383-0e6b-5caf-9485-668de5347ff7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af3cc332-417c-564a-9706-84c0a8c01993" :answer/id #uuid "af3cc332-417c-564a-9706-84c0a8c01993" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8731c983-97f9-53c1-8f4f-50deb4aa8e18" :answer/id #uuid "8731c983-97f9-53c1-8f4f-50deb4aa8e18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7172c2f1-854e-5f7a-bb0a-b29292218c1d" :answer/id #uuid "7172c2f1-854e-5f7a-bb0a-b29292218c1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186395024 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $r=sv+tw$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -16 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 26 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-db652323-dfa8-59de-a50e-77465a8c2c78" :question/id #uuid "db652323-dfa8-59de-a50e-77465a8c2c78" :question/math-academy-id "q-95199" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $r=sv+tw$, where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $r=sv+tw$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fecc41d3-c390-5acf-8b50-f50b7922b93c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fecc41d3-c390-5acf-8b50-f50b7922b93c" :answer-field/id #uuid "fecc41d3-c390-5acf-8b50-f50b7922b93c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-701637cb-ebaf-5484-9c58-0f6178d164d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-701637cb-ebaf-5484-9c58-0f6178d164d0" "answer-b3a65d0b-defa-5133-81fa-07a1e31fc778" "answer-05a4f965-cd55-59e6-8c4b-757ba68f6ffb" "answer-a5f0ca7f-1d5a-55cd-bb1e-8efad02dd4e6" "answer-b2c45046-1f3d-5d15-8596-658ef1926dc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-701637cb-ebaf-5484-9c58-0f6178d164d0" :answer/id #uuid "701637cb-ebaf-5484-9c58-0f6178d164d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3a65d0b-defa-5133-81fa-07a1e31fc778" :answer/id #uuid "b3a65d0b-defa-5133-81fa-07a1e31fc778" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligne